{
  "summary": "This study investigates engineering a plant-inspired cell armor to enable red blood cell lyophilization. Using a biomimetic approach, the researchers developed an artificial membrane armor (AMA) combining ZIF-8 for mechanical support, a water-retentive polymer matrix, and Zn2+ coordination to maintain membrane fluidity. The AMA achieved ∼95% post-rehydration recovery with minimal protectant content and a 3-month shelf life, preserving RBC functionality without cold-chain requirements.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The biomimetic cell armor enabled ∼95% post-rehydration recovery of red blood cells after lyophilization",
    "The armor achieved an ultra-low protectant content (<5 wt%) and ambient shelf stability for at least 3 months",
    "Rehydrated red blood cells maintained native morphology, membrane mechanics, metabolic activity, and oxygen transport capability"
  ],
  "study_type": "Biomimetic engineering",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a transferable strategy for imparting desiccation tolerance from plant cell walls to mammalian cells, enabling ambient-temperature storage of sensitive biological systems without cold-chain dependence."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates programmable electronic structures in carbon nanostructures for efficient near-infrared photocatalytic hydrogen peroxide generation. By engineering framework-derived carbon with tunable electronic states, researchers identified an intermediate semi-graphitized phase that optimizes near-infrared absorption, charge separation, and selective two-electron oxygen reduction. The system enables NIR-driven H2:O2 production without sacrificial agents, facilitating a self-amplifying redox cascade for cancer therapy with significant in vivo tumor suppression.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Intermediate semi-graphitized carbon phase maximizes near-infrared absorption and selective two-electron oxygen reduction",
    "Non-monotonic activity dependence on graphitization degree with optimal performance at intermediate stages",
    "NIR-driven H2O2 generation enables self-amplifying redox cascade for cancer therapy without sacrificial agents"
  ],
  "study_type": "Photocatalytic carbon nanostructure engineering",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes an electronic-structure paradigm for carbon-based photocatalysis, enabling selective two-electron oxygen reduction under physiological conditions and providing a generalizable strategy for modulating reaction efficiency in metal-free systems."
}

{
  "summary": "The study used CRISPR mutagenesis to map functional elements in the MYC locus at base-pair resolution, revealing that 67% of essential base pairs are noncoding. It identified an ultraconserved noncoding RNA element in the MYC 3' UTR that regulates mRNA and protein localization via hnRNP L interaction. Steric-blocking ASOs targeting this element selectively kill MYC-dependent cancer cells and inhibit tumor growth, highlighting noncoding sequences as critical functional carriers and identifying a druggable target.",
  "key_findings": [
    "67% of functionally essential base pairs in the MYC locus are noncoding",
    "An ultraconserved noncoding RNA element in the MYC 3' UTR promotes perinuclear mRNA localization and nuclear protein localization via hnRNP L interaction",
    "Steric-blocking ASOs targeting this RNA element selectively eliminate MYC-dependent cancer cells and inhibit patient-derived tumor organoid growth"
  ],
  "study_type": "CRISPR tiling mutagenesis",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates that noncoding sequences are major carriers of functional genetic information and identifies a druggable RNA element that disrupts MYC-driven cancer mechanisms through targeted therapeutic intervention."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines ultralow-power transistor technologies beyond the 60 mV/dec Boltzmann limit for sub-2 nm CMOS scaling. It analyzes tunnel field-effect transistors (TFETs), negative-capacitance field-effect transistors (NCFETs), and cold-source field-effect transistors (CSFETs), discussing their physical principles, recent advances, optimization strategies, and remaining challenges for future integration with advanced CMOS nodes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "TFETs, NCFETs, and CSFETs are the primary sub-60 mV/dec technologies addressing CMOS scaling bottlenecks",
    "The review details underlying physical principles and optimization strategies for these ultralow-power transistors",
    "Future breakthroughs require novel materials, multi-mechanism integration, and experimental verification with advanced CMOS nodes"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This review provides a comprehensive analysis of emerging ultralow-power transistor technologies that could overcome the fundamental Boltzmann thermal limit, enabling further scaling of sub-2 nm CMOS transistors through novel device physics."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates a pathology-adaptive hydrogel for autoimmune uveitis treatment by targeting stage-specific pathological mechanisms. The composite hydrogel delivers minocycline and CD44-targeted curcumin nanoparticles to suppress early microglial activation and block advanced-stage inflammatory cell infiltration. In EAU rats, it effectively stabilizes blood-retinal barriers and reduces inflammation without systemic side effects.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Single subconjunctival administration of Mino&nanoCur@PLEL hydrogel suppresses early microglial overactivation in EAU rats",
    "Sustained nanoCur release targets proinflammatory CD4:44+ macrophages to protect blood-retinal barrier integrity",
    "The hydrogel disrupts bidirectional crosstalk between immune cells and blood-retinal barriers, restoring retinal immune homeostasis"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo rat model of experimental autoimmune uveitis",
  "scientific_significance": "This approach provides a stage-specific immunomodulatory strategy for autoimmune uveitis by synchronizing therapeutic interventions with temporal disease progression stages."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of comparing sample-level heterogeneity in single-cell genomics by introducing scSLIDE, a framework that generates compact embeddings of sample-specific cell distributions. Applied to COVID-19, Alzheimer's disease, and zebrafish embryogenesis, scSLIDE identifies patient subgroups, disease trajectories, and coordinated cellular programs across samples, revealing separable axes of infection severity, Alzheimer's progression, and embryonic pseudostages.",
  "key_findings": [
    "scSLIDE identifies separable axes of infection and severity in COVID-19",
    "scSLIDE reconstructs a trajectory of Alzheimer's disease progression",
    "scSLIDE reveals embryonic 'pseudostages' that vary across and within time points in zebrafish embryogenesis"
  ],
  "study_type": "Computational framework for single-cell data analysis",
  "scientific_significance": "scSLIDE provides a method to characterize sample-level biological variation in single-cell data, enabling the reconstruction of disease trajectories and cellular programs across diverse biological contexts without collapsing heterogeneity into binary case-control labels."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates dipicolinic acid-anchored self-assembled monolayers (SAMs) for enhancing perovskite photovoltaics. The researchers engineered XS21, a multidentate dipicolinic acid-based SAM, to improve substrate binding and interfacial passivation. The material demonstrated high power conversion efficiency (21.54% in single-junction cells) and exceptional stability in tandem configurations (29.62% PCE with >90% retention after 900 h).",
  "key_findings": [
    "XS21 SAMs achieve 21.54% power conversion efficiency in wide-bandgap perovskite solar cells",
    "XS21 enables monolithic tandem perovskite solar cells with 29.62% power conversion efficiency",
    "XS21 provides superior UV photostability and operational stability (>90% efficiency retention after 900 h)"
  ],
  "study_type": "Experimental",
  "scientific_significance": "The development of multidentate dipicolinic acid anchoring provides a robust strategy for enhancing both efficiency and stability in perovskite photovoltaics, addressing critical interfacial challenges in next-generation solar cell design."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated a strategy to counteract bone loss during orthodontic tooth movement in periodontitis by developing hydrogel microspheres that buffer mechanical stress and neutralize reactive oxygen species (ROS). The microspheres, composed of dopamine-modified hyaluronic acid and phenylboronic acid-modified gelatin, preserved dental follicle stem cell viability and function under mechanoinflammatory conditions, preventing bone loss and restoring osteoimmune homeostasis in murine models.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Microspheres dissipated mechanical stress and scavenged ROS to protect dental follicle stem cells",
    "The microspheres maintained stem cell viability, osteogenic potential, and immunomodulatory function",
    "Transcriptomic analysis showed upregulation of DNA repair enzyme OGG1, suppressing NF-κB inflammation"
  ],
  "study_type": "Murine model of periodontitis-associated orthodontic tooth movement",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a biomaterial-based approach to maintain stem cell function and osteoimmune balance in mechanically and immunologically stressed tissues, offering a potential mechanism to prevent bone loss during orthodontic treatment in periodontitis."
}

{
  "summary": "This study identifies ZmNAET1 as a critical transporter for nickel allocation to maize seeds via nicotianamine-mediated phloem loading. Genome-wide association analysis revealed that ZmNA.ET1 mutants show reduced yield, seed germination, and longevity, with nickel supplementation rescuing these defects. The findings highlight nickel's role in amino acid turnover for grain development in cereals.",
  "key_findings": [
    "ZmNAET1 is a key transporter for nickel allocation to maize seeds",
    "ZmNAET1 mutants exhibit yield penalties, reduced seed germination, and shortened seed longevity",
    "Nickel supplementation rescues seed germination and vegetative development in mutants"
  ],
  "study_type": "Genome-wide association analysis",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes nickel's essential role in cereals through nicotianamine-dependent transport, revealing a previously underappreciated mechanism for yield formation in grain crops."
}

{
  "summary": "This study uses transient absorption spectroscopy to distinguish between noninteracting and interacting Y6 chromophores in solution and thin films. It identifies localized Frenkel excitons (LE) in noninteracting Y6 and aggregate excitons (AE) in interacting Y6, with AEs showing fluence-dependent lifetimes due to singlet-singlet annihilation. Long-lived polaronic states form on aggregated Y6 within tens of picoseconds, persisting into the nanosecond regime. The findings suggest that polaron formation in Y6 films arises from an Onsager-like autoionization process of delocalized excitons in interacting domains.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Localized Frenkel-type excitons (LE) decay monoexponentially on nanosecond timescales in noninteracting Y6",
    "Aggregate excitons (AE) exhibit fluence-dependent lifetimes (30-200 ps) governed by singlet-singlet annihilation",
    "Long-lived polaronic states form on aggregated Y6 within tens of picoseconds and persist into the nanosecond regime"
  ],
  "study_type": "Transient absorption spectroscopy",
  "scientific_significance": "The study provides a framework for interpreting transient absorption features in Y6 and identifies the Onsager-like autoionization mechanism underlying polaron formation in interacting domains, advancing understanding of charge generation in nonfullerene acceptor photovoltaics."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how a bone-mimetic scaffold creates an ectopic osteo-immune niche to protect against osteoporosis. The scaffold induces a structured foreign body response that recruits host cells into a compartmentalized immune microenvironment rich in FOXP3+ regulatory T cells. In an oste:porosis model, it reduced systemic bone loss and increased Treg populations, indicating that local niche engineering can systemically regulate skeletal health.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Subcutaneously implanted bone-mimetic scaffolds generate ectopic osteo-immune niches",
    "These niches feature FOXP3+ regulatory T cell enrichment and structured immune microenvironments",
    "The approach attenuates systemic bone loss in osteoporosis models through Treg-mediated immunoregulation"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes that localized biomaterial engineering can systemically modulate immune regulation to protect against osteoporosis, offering a novel therapeutic strategy for osteo-immune disorders."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates COP1's role as a temperature-responsive switch in Arabid. The research demonstrates that COP1 undergoes a conformational change at warmer temperatures, where an intramolecular Val-Pro motif detaches from its active site, enabling client protein degradation. This mechanism allows plants to precisely sense temperature within climate ranges, with modifications to the VP motif altering the temperature response range.",
  "key_findings": [
    "COP1 acts as a temperature-dependent switch via a conformational change",
    "An intramolecular Val-Pro motif prevents substrate docking at low temperatures",
    "Detachment of the VP motif at warmer temperatures enables client protein degradation"
  ],
  "study_type": "Mechanistic study",
  "scientific_significance": "This work reveals a molecular mechanism for temperature sensing in plants, providing insight into how temperature ranges are precisely regulated within climatic conditions."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates a simple geometric descriptor, ∆d, for predicting ion migration barriers in solid-state ionic conductors across multiple chemist: Li-, Na-, K-, and Mg-ions in oxides, sulfides, and halides. By analyzing migration barriers, ∆d captures short-range repulsion penalties and distinguishes low- from high-barrier pathways. It enables high-throughput screening of conductors and links diverse transport mechanisms to a common physical quantity.",
  "key_findings": [
    "∆d measures the largest contraction of mobile-ion-neighbor distances relative to equilibrium values along migration pathways",
    "∆d consistently distinguishes low- from high-barrier pathways across different ion chemistries and material classes",
    "High-throughput screening using ∆d identifies known fast-ion conductors and previously unexplored candidates validated by simulations"
  ],
  "study_type": "Computational screening and descriptor development",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a transferable geometric descriptor that unifies ion migration barrier prediction across diverse mobile-ion chemistries and material classes, advancing the rational design of solid-state ionic conductors."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated how a mitochondrial-protective polysaccharide (colanic acid) improves burn wound healing by modulating immune-metabolic interactions. Using a murine burn model, CA enhanced wound closure 3.1-fold, reduced oxidative stress 54%, and increased collagen deposition 193%. Mechanistically, CA internalized via TLR4, restored mitochondrial function, and promoted macrophage M2 polarization through metabolic reprogramming, outperforming hyaluronic acid dressings in healing.",
  "key_findings": [
    "CA enhanced wound closure 3.1-fold in murine deep second-degree burns",
    "CA reduced reactive oxidative species by 54% and increased mature collagen deposition by 193%",
    "CA internalized via TLR4, restored mitochondrial cristae structure, and elevated ATP production 1.5-fold"
  ],
  "study_type": "Murine deep second-degree burn model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates that mitochondrial-protective polysaccharides can reprogram immune-metabolic crosstalk to accelerate burn wound repair, providing a mechanistic basis for targeting mitochondrial dysfunction in tissue regeneration."
}

{
  "summary": "This study used bioorthogonal proteomics to investigate blood-brain barrier protein transport in healthy and Alzheimer's disease models. It found the healthy BBB samples 191 plasma proteins and permits 92 into the brain, enriched for carrier and metabolic functions. Early Alzheimer's models show selective remodeling of protein transport with reduced APOA1 and increased SERPINA1 uptake, while hepatic APOA1 overexpression rescues deficits in these models. The findings highlight the BBB's role in selectively recruiting neuroprotective proteins for brain health.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The healthy blood-brain barrier samples 191 plasma proteins and permits 92 into the brain parenchyma",
    "Early-stage Alzheimer's disease models exhibit reduced APOA1 and increased SERPINA1 uptake at the blood-brain barrier",
    "Hepatic APOA1 overexpression rescues astrocytic, synaptic, and behavioral deficits in Alzheimer's disease models"
  ],
  "study_type": "Bioorthogonal proteomics",
  "scientific_significance": "This study demonstrates the blood-brain barrier's selective recruitment of neuroprotective circulatory proteins to maintain brain health, with implications for understanding early Alzheimer's disease pathophysiology and potential therapeutic strategies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates enhancing the calendar life of lithium metal batteries by developing a durable solid-electrolyte interphase (SEI) through cross-linked polymer integration. The approach combines inorganics with a high-modulus, low-swelling polymer to improve mechanical stability and suppress electrolyte permeation. Results show a 73% increase in cycle life and 129% extension in calendar life for coin cells, with a pouch cell achieving 124 cycles.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Cross-linked polymer integration enhances SEI mechanical stability and passivation capability",
    "SEI elastic modulus increased from 1.63 to 4.37 GPa",
    "Calendar life extended by 129% (from 1553 to 3561 hours) for Li metal coin cells"
  ],
  "study_type": "In situ SEI modification",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a strategy to concurrently improve SEI mechanical resilience and electrolyte barrier properties, addressing the critical challenge of calendar life degradation in lithium metal batteries."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated engineering hypoactivated platelet-derived coatings (hPLC) to address thrombosis and delayed healing in vascular stents. By sequentially assembling fibrin networks and low-activated platelets followed by controlled lysis, hPLC decouples thrombogenic activity from regenerative functions. In vivo results showed accelerated re-endothelialization, reduced neointimal hyperplasia, and suppressed peri-strut inflammation, indicating hPLC's potential for improving vascular regeneration.",
  "key_findings": [
    "hPLC preserves platelet membrane structure while reducing thrombogenicity",
    "hPLC-coated stents accelerate re-endothelialization and inhibit neointimal hyperplasia",
    "Transcriptomic analysis reveals enhanced endothelial compatibility via anti-apoptotic and angiogenic pathways"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo stent implantation studies",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a bioinspired strategy for engineering multifunctional vascular interfaces using blood-derived components, providing a clinically translatable approach to decouple thrombosis and promote vascular regeneration."
}

{
  "summary": "This study describes the NIH BRAIN Initiative Cell Atlas Network (BICAN) collaboration, which integrates multi-journal publications to establish a comprehensive human brain reference framework. The approach combines efforts across Cell, Cell Genomics, Cell Reports, Cell Reports Methods, Cell Systems, and Neuron to create a unified reference map of the human brain's cellular composition.",
  "key_findings": [
    "BICAN represents the first coordinated effort to generate a comprehensive human brain reference framework",
    "The initiative integrates data and methodologies from multiple Cell family journals",
    "The framework aims to provide a standardized cellular atlas for the human brain"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a foundational reference framework for the human brain, advancing the field by enabling systematic, cross-scale integration of cellular data to support future neuroscience research."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the electronic structure of the layered altermagnet CoNb4Se8 using termination-resolved micro-ARPES, spin-ARPES, and DFT. It resolves spin-split branches, demonstrates spin polarization reversal across time-reversal domains, and identifies a low-temperature anomaly near 50-6. The findings establish a momentum-resolved benchmark for layered altermagnets.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Direct observation of spin-split branches in raw photoemission spectra of CoNb4Se8",
    "Reversal of spin polarization at opposite momenta and between sample regions, indicating time-reversal domains",
    "Weak low-temperature anomaly near 50-60 K detected via magnetometry and micro-ARPES"
  ],
  "study_type": "Termination-resolved micro-ARPES, spin-ARPES, and density functional theory",
  "scientific_significance": "The study provides a momentum-resolved benchmark for understanding electronic structures in layered altermagnets, advancing knowledge of noncollinear magnetic phases with momentum-dependent spin splitting."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated enhancing bioprosthetic heart valve longevity by integrating immunomodulation and endothelialization. Using metal-polyphenol hydrogel nanoparticles with antioxidant and anti-inflammatory properties, the researchers created a biomimetic barrier that resists thrombosis, reduces inflammation, and promotes endothelial cell adhesion. The coating also catalyzed nitric oxide release to support vascular health, addressing key failure mechanisms in current bioprosthetic valves.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Metal-polyphenol hydrogel nanoparticles with antioxidant and immunomodulatory functions were engineered to coat bioprosthetic heart valves",
    "The coating reduced inflammation and reactive oxygen species while promoting endothelial cell adhesion and growth",
    "Nitric oxide release via PI3K/AKT/mTOR pathway enhanced endothelialization and vascular homeostasis"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates a biomimetic strategy to simultaneously address thrombosis, inflammation, and endothelialization challenges in bioprosthetic heart valves, offering a potential pathway for improved long-term durability through integrated immunomodulatory and vascular protective mechanisms."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates age-related changes in the primate brain across cell types, regions, and sexes using a single-nucleus atlas of macaque brain tissue. It identifies context-dependent aging programs, sex-biased transcriptional aging, and molecular connections to Alzheimer's disease, providing insights into aging mechanisms in primates.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Context-dependent aging programs in the macaque brain",
    "Sex-biased transcriptional aging patterns",
    "Molecular links between aging and Alzheimer's disease"
  ],
  "study_type": "Single-nucleus atlas of macaque brain tissue",
  "scientific_significance": "The study advances understanding of aging mechanisms in primates by revealing how aging processes vary across cellular contexts, sexes, and disease relevance."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether chiral phonons in non-magnetic ferroelectrics can drive ultrafast spin currents. Using broadband terahertz emission spectroscopy, researchers observed spin currents in In2Se3 following ultrafast laser excitation, attributing the effect to a chiral phonon-induced spin Seebeck mechanism. The results demonstrate controllable ter:ellipticity and sign reversal with sample orientation or material changes, suggesting chiral phonons enable ultrafast spin current generation without magnetic materials.",
  "key_findings": [
    "In2Se3 generates ultrafast spin currents via chiral phonon-induced spin Seebeck effect after laser excitation",
    "Terahertz emission shows sign reversal with sample orientation or Pt/W overlayer substitution",
    "Polarization-resolved measurements reveal controllable terahertz ellipticity indicating polarization-dependent ultrafast response"
  ],
  "study_type": "Broadband terahertz emission spectroscopy",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes chiral phonons as a mechanism for generating ultrafast spin currents in non-magnetic materials, offering a new pathway for terahertz sources with switchable handedness without magnetic components."
}

{
  "summary": "The study investigated the role of Myc in ocular neovascularization (NV) using endothelial-specific Myc silencing. Researchers identified Myc upregulation in neovascular endothelial cells (NV-ECs) driven by glycolytic reprogramming and hexosamine biosynthetic pathway (HBP) activity. They engineered an endothelium-targeted lipid nanoparticle (Lip@siM. The nanoparticle achieved targeted delivery, sustained Myc silencing, and significantly inhibited NV in mice with efficacy comparable to aflibercept.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Myc was markedly upregulated in neovascular endothelial cells (NV-ECs) in ocular NV",
    "Glycolytic reprogramming activated the hexosamine biosynthetic pathway (HBP), promoting Myc O-GlcNAcylation and stabilization",
    "Endothelium-targeted lipid nanoparticles with Myc siRNA (Lip@siMyc) suppressed NV progression with efficacy comparable to aflibercept"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo mouse model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies a glycolysis/HBP/Myc axis as a critical regulator of endothelial cell proliferation in ocular neovascularization, providing a novel therapeutic target for NV treatment."
}

